     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · BBC World · 2026-10-26 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Anthropic Warns of AI 'Catastrophic Risk' Ahead of Nasdaq Listing</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#AI</span> <span>#Anthropic</span> <span>#IPO</span> <span>#Technology</span></div>
       <p>Anthropic, the AI firm behind the Claude chatbot, has warned of potential &quot;catastrophic or existential risks to humanity&quot; from advanced artificial intelligence systems as it unveiled plans for a blockbuster stock market listing in the US. The company expects its valuation to surpass SpaceX's initial $1.8 trillion listing and will be the first fully AI-focused firm to go public, listing on Nasdaq under the symbol ANT.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/anthropic-warns-of-ai-catastrophic-risk-ahead-of-nasdaq-list.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The firm reported $16.3 billion in revenue during the first half of the year, alongside losses exceeding $80.7 billion. Anthropic stated that advanced AI systems, if not developed with appropriate safeguards, oversight, and security, could pose significant dangers, especially if misused, replicated, weaponized, or allowed to operate with high autonomy. These concerns have been raised by policymakers, regulators, researchers, and even its own staff.</p><p>Founded five years ago by Dario Amodei, Daniela Amodei, and five others, Anthropic's announcement in June was quickly followed by rival OpenAI's similar listing intentions for early next year. The sector has also seen a wave of cyber-security breaches involving AI agents. Additionally, the owners of Elvis Presley's music rights have sued Anthropic, alleging the firm used hundreds of the artist's songs to train its Claude chatbot. Anthropic also warned investors of its reliance on a cluster of cloud computing providers, Taiwanese chipmakers, and US-concentrated data centers, alongside potential regulatory actions for harms associated with its AI models.</p><p>Despite the risks, Anthropic envisions AI unlocking &quot;millions of virtual agents&quot; to revolutionize human endeavors and profoundly accelerate progress in biology and medicine, potentially finding cures for diseases like cancer and Alzheimer's, and boosting economic growth. Analysts like Matt Orton of Raymond James Investment noted that rapid growth and financial losses are expected for such firms, while Peter Andersen of Andersen Capital Management called the listing a &quot;significant milestone&quot; for AI development.</p><p>What to watch: The market's reception to Anthropic's debut will indicate investor sentiment towards AI's future.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes all key information from the full text, including financial figures, risks, and expert commentary.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cq398p1wd1evo?at_medium=RSS&amp;at_campaign=rss" rel="noopener">BBC World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
