     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-26 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Building a PC to Match Xbox Series X Remains Costly</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Building a PC capable of matching the $799.99 Xbox Series X remains significantly more expensive than purchasing the console, with new parts costing approximately $1,200 including Windows. While the price gap has narrowed since 2020, even a budget build using used components costs roughly $730.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/building-a-pc-to-match-xbox-series-x-remains-costly.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Microsoft recently increased console prices due to rising memory and storage costs, which have surged over 2.5 times since early 2023. Although modern entry-level GPUs like the Intel Arc B580 and RX 7600 offer performance comparable to the console's internal hardware, the total expenditure for a new PC build continues to exceed the cost of the hardware.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Clear and accurate reporting on the cost comparison between console and PC building as provided in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2294342/how-much-build-pc-similar-xbox-series-x/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
