     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-26 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Comparing Starlink and 5G Home Internet Performance</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Selecting between Starlink satellite broadband and 5G home internet depends heavily on location, signal strength, and network availability. While Starlink Residential Max plans advertise speeds over 400 Mbps and 5G providers like T-Mobile report averages up to 498 Mbps, real-world performance varies significantly based on time of day, equipment, and local infrastructure.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/comparing-starlink-and-5g-home-internet-performance.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Independent testing shows Starlink speeds fluctuate between 20 Mbps and 250 Mbps in rural areas, while 5G networks often deliver higher average download rates depending on the carrier and spectrum access. Beyond download speeds, users must evaluate upload capabilities and latency, which are critical for connection stability and file transfers.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately synthesizes the performance comparison between Starlink and 5G, noting the variables in real-world application.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2294294/how-starlink-speeds-compare-5g/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
