     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-26 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New South Wales legalizes 'human composting'</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New South Wales has become the first Australian state to legalize human composting, formally known as natural organic reduction or terramation. This process utilizes microorganisms to break down human remains into compost, offering a sustainable alternative to traditional burial and cremation.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/new-south-wales-legalizes-human-composting.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Decomposition naturally occurs as microorganisms and insects consume soft tissues, though environmental conditions significantly impact the timeline. Research indicates that burial methods, such as the use of sealed stone tombs versus soil types, can cause decomposition to take anywhere from less than a decade to over 40 years. This new legislative option addresses growing concerns regarding environmental impact and limited cemetery space.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately covers the new legislation in NSW with exact details from the text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-human-composting.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
