     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-10-26 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>SHRC Directs Health Department Review After Boy's Death in Kannur Clinic</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#SHRC</span> <span>#Kerala Health</span> <span>#Private Clinics</span> <span>#Kannur</span></div>
       <p>The Kerala State Human Rights Commission (SHRC) has issued a directive to the Health department, demanding stringent checks on private clinical establishments following the death of a 17-year-old boy after tonsil surgery in Kannur in July 2024. The Commission instructed the Additional Chief Secretary, Health department, to ensure private clinics adhere to prescribed standards and to take legal action against any violators.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/shrc-directs-health-department-review-after-boys-death-in-ka.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The directive specifically calls for authorities to verify the qualifications and registration of doctors and other staff working at these clinics. Additionally, the SHRC has urged the formulation of guidelines to ensure the quality of medical services and patient safety, alongside the implementation of an effective monitoring mechanism.</p><p>This order stems from a complaint filed by P. Shaji, the boy’s father and a native of Kannadiparamba, whose son died on July 17, 2024. An inquiry conducted by the Commission’s own wing, headed by its Inspector General, revealed significant lapses. The investigation found that the doctor who performed the surgery lacked the legal authorisation to practise in Keralam, and neither the clinic where the surgery occurred nor the facility to which the boy was subsequently admitted had obtained the required registration under state rules.</p><p>Furthermore, the SHRC has directed the State Police Chief to order a comprehensive further investigation into the case registered by the Kannur Town police. This probe is to be supervised by a Crime Branch officer not below the rank of Deputy Superintendent of Police. The investigation will thoroughly examine alleged treatment lapses, claims of alterations to medical and nursing records, the operating doctor's qualifications and registration, and the registration status of the clinical establishments involved. The Commission also noted discrepancies between reports from inquiries led by the district medical officer and observed &quot;culpable negligence&quot; in the boy's death.</p><p>What to watch: The progress of the police investigation and clinic compliance checks.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures all essential details, sequence of events, and specific directives from the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/kerala/shrc-calls-for-checks-on-private-clinics-after-boys-death-following-tonsil-surgery-in-kannur/article71628110.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
