     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-26 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study finds scientists' research impact shifts over career span</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A study of 11.2 million papers published by over 200,000 scientists reveals that research disruption patterns evolve with experience. While younger scientists are more likely to produce highly disruptive work that challenges existing paradigms, the probability of creating persistently disruptive research increases throughout a career.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/study-finds-scientists-research-impact-shifts-over-career-sp.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers from the Chinese Academy of Sciences found that the likelihood of producing a persistently disruptive paper more than doubles from 1% in early years to 2.2% after 40 years of experience. This shift is attributed to cumulative research experience rather than biological age, though mathematics and computer science deviate from this observed trend.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures the study's numerical findings and nuances precisely.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-scientists-disruptive-age-breakthroughs.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
