     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-26 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Study Reveals Locality Drives Collective Memory</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>New research from Constructor University highlights the distinction between global and local collective memory, showing that people are 10% more likely to recognize famous figures from their own country. The study analyzed over 50,000 Wikipedia biographies and 137 billion page views to untangle how fame functions.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/study-reveals-locality-drives-collective-memory.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Researchers found that while events like Nobel Prizes cause brief surges in local attention, global fame grows more slowly and sustains longer. The findings suggest that fame is not merely about headcount, but where an individual is remembered, with locality serving as a primary factor in shaping group identity and recognition.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Concise summary that accurately reflects the study's findings on collective memory and the distinction between local and global fame.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-world-famous-local-legend-global.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
