     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-10-26 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Tel Aviv University Study Evaluates Prediction Market Accuracy</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>A Tel Aviv University study published in PNAS analyzed over one million forecasts across four major prediction platforms to assess collective accuracy. Researchers examined 12 domains, including geopolitics and technology, over a decade to determine if crowds can reliably predict future events.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-26/tel-aviv-university-study-evaluates-prediction-market-accura.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Findings indicate that while collective forecasts are often remarkably close, their reliability varies significantly by topic. The study concludes that prediction markets do not guarantee certainty but offer valuable insights into potential outcomes, provided users understand the systemic limitations and biases inherent in how different events are estimated.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief clearly summarizes the study's scope and findings as reported in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-10-future-million-clues-certainty.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
